WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
SPEAKER: Providing a good
user experience for consumers

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.620
of your API is crucial.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.700
This work continues even
after you've intentionally

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.390
built your API with simple
and intuitive design.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:15.120
It's also equally important
to enable developers

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.180
with self-service tools
to easily find and learn

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.640
how to use your API
so they can ultimately

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.060
build their app using your
APIs quickly and securely.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.840
Apigee's configurable developer
portal enables app developers

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.049
with API discovery, exploration,
learning, and testing.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:34.050
And so in this episode,
we will learn more

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.460
about the developer portal.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.560
Whether the APIs you build are
for internal or external use,

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.140
the developer portal
enables API developers

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.920
to publish your catalog
of API products,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.820
offer detailed documentation
for each of those products,

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.020
create an easy app developer
onboarding process,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.970
have mechanisms to create
an app and an API key.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:05.580
You can even try out your API.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.830
You can also leverage
the default templates

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.930
or customize the
portal's styling

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.910
to suit your company's branding.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.130
And what's nice is
that it's connected

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.800
to your Apigee console,
which centralizes

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.090
all your API management
tasks in one place, including

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.080
API analytics, which we will
dive in another episode.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.200
Now let's take a quick tour
from both a publisher's view

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.440
at setting up the portal, as
well as the consumer's view

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.150
accessing the portal.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.040
In prior episodes,
we talked about how

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.570
to create a Cloud project,
enable Apigee APIs, creating

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:43.440
an evaluation org, then
log into Apigee.google.com,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.190
build your API Proxy, and later
bundle one or more proxies

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.840
into an API product.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.570
Now to create a portal
from the same console,

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.300
visit Publish, Portals,
the plus Portal

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.440
button to create a new portal.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.972
Since I already have one, I will
click Cancel and use this one.

00:01:59.972 --> 00:02:01.680
Next, we will see the
different items you

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.270
can configure in your portal.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.700
Now we added an
existing API product

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.419
by selecting API Catalog, the
plus sign in the top corner,

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:11.460
and then select an
API product, then

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.130
select Next to go to
the API Details page.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:15.960
You also need to
check the box called

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.140
Publish to be able to be
listed in the catalog.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.380
Next, you need to associate
the documentation for that API

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.250
product.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.130
The format of the documentation
is an open API specification,

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:28.680
which is an industry standard.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.110
In this demo, I already created
one for this simple proxy.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.490
You can then add an image
and associate categories

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.520
that help make the API
searchable on the portal.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.200
Then click Save.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.540
Once you have added the product
and publish the documentation,

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.720
you can actually visit
the developer portal

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.020
to see what it looks like
by selecting the Live Portal

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.010
link to launch it.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.110
This is a developer
portal that Apigee

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.610
provides to get you started.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.110
And to make changes to
the pages and theme,

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.890
you can return to your
portal's configuration options,

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.460
and then choose a page--
for example, Get Started.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.680
This will open the
page editor where

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.250
you can make changes using
markdown's simple formatting,

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.740
which is then converted
to HTML to render

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.260
the page in your browser.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.750
Now let's see the view
from an external app

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.910
developer visiting the portal.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.270
App developers first sign up
to create an account and login,

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.960
so they can create an
app and get an API key.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.480
After that, this
is the landing page

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.550
for a consumer app developer.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.860
They can see all the
API products available

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.960
and explore their details.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.970
I can view those endpoints
to understand how they work,

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:40.200
and I can actually execute
a call to try it out.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.930
If I don't have an API
key, I get an error.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.180
To create my own
API key, I visit

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.260
New app, Enter App Details,
and enable the API product I

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.810
wish to use, and click Create.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.750
Now Apigee registered my
app and assigned a key

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.940
to it, which I can quickly copy.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.210
Now let's go back into the API
product and test out the key.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.140
Click Authorize.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.150
And from the
dropdown, you can see

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.460
that it knows I already
created an app, which

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.070
I select and click Authorize.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.900
Now I can make a
call, which you can

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.010
see it points to my demo app
inside of my evaluation org

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:16.709
and is using this product.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:17.399
Great.

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:19.740
So to wrap up, let's
do a small recap.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.530
Apigee provides a
configurable app developer

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.780
portal out of the
box, which is publicly

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.170
available on the internet where
developers can find your API

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.420
catalog, sign up,
register their app

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.870
to get an API key to
use with the APIs.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.700
The interactive
API documentation

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.490
allows developers to test out
the API while they're also

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.440
developing their app.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.080
If you found this episode
helpful, community,

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.020
you can subscribe to
learn about more episodes

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.810
on getting started with Apigee's
API management tool set.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:50.310
Cheers.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

